- op: discuss  # 5866
  at: '2026-03-19T02:32:37Z'  # 5866
  by: agent  # 5866
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5866
  clock: 6  # 5866
  nonce: '5866'  # 5866
  message: "Function pointer resolution in C is well-studied but not commonly implemented in static analyzers at the level we need:\n\n**What language servers do:**\n- **clangd/clang-tidy**: Full type-aware analysis. Resolves function pointers through type inference and dataflow. But requires a compile_commands.json (build system integration). Not practical for repos analyzed without building.\n- **cquery/ccls**: Similar to clangd — build-dependent.\n\n**What static analyzers do:**\n- **Infer (Facebook)**: Resolves function pointers for null-safety and thread-safety checks. Requires build integration.\n- **Cppcheck**: Limited function pointer tracking. Heuristic-based.\n- **tree-sitter based**: No existing tool does function pointer resolution from tree-sitter ASTs alone.\n\n**Would this be novel?** Doing function pointer resolution from tree-sitter (without build system) would be a genuine contribution. The approach would be heuristic: match struct field types to function signatures, track assignments in initialization code. Not as precise as clangd, but useful for the 'good enough' level hypergumbo targets.\n\n**Recommendation:** Start with the heuristic approach (match function pointer assignments by name/signature similarity). This handles the common pattern where callback structs are initialized with named functions (e.g., `.connect = my_connect_func`)."  # 5866
